implying that $x_{v_2}\ge x_{v_1}$, from which, we have  $x_{u_k}\ge x_{u_i}$. So $i=3$, $k=2$ and $j=1$.
As $G$ is $K_{1,4}$-free, $d_I(v_1)=1$ and $d_G(u_3)=2$, we have $d_I(v)\le2$ for any $v\in K\setminus\{v_0,v_1\}$.
Together with $x_{u_1}\ge x_{u_2}$ and $|I|\ge4$, we have $N_G(u_2)\ne K\setminus \{v_1\}$.
So  $G-u_3v_1+u_2v_1\in\mathbb{G}_n$, and by Lemma \ref{perron}, we have $\rho(G-u_3v_1+u_2v_1)>\rho$, a contradiction.




Suppose next that $d_I(v_1)\ge2$ and $N_I(v_1)\subseteq\{u_1,u_2,u_3\}$.
We claim that $x_{u}\ge x_{u_i}$ for any $u\in I\setminus\{u_1,u_2,u_3\}$, where the equality holds only if $d_G(u)=2$. It is obvious if $d_G(u)=2$.
